SWEET AKS: a real software passcode, with no SEP (2321)

The stub of 0038/0238 answers "no passcode, always unlocked" to everything, so
iOS thinks a passcode is set (`sls 0`) and the Passcode pane misbehaves. This
gives AppleKeyStore a real verifier instead:

- PBKDF2-HMAC-SHA256, 100000 rounds, 16-byte salt, in /private/var/db/sweet-passcode
  as two checksummed 96-byte slots one 4 KiB block apart. A missing or corrupt
  file means "no passcode", so a bad file can never lock the owner out.
- The failed-try count is in its own file and is fsynced BEFORE the verifier
  runs, so a reset mid-guess still counts it. Backoff is 5 free tries, then
  60/300/900/900 s, then 3600 s forever. Never a wipe.
- Real lock state in selectors 7, 8, 9, 12, 13, 17, 32 and 35, and the code
  changes in 5 and 15 behind com.apple.keystore.device.
- The lock-state IOKit interest messages 0xe3ffd00c..0xe3ffd013, which the stub
  never sent and SpringBoard needs.
- Selector 42 verifies. P0 on 2026-11-04 proved it arrives as FIVE SCALARS and
  no structure input, so the packed buffer is a bare pointer in scalar 2 with
  its length in scalar 3. Selector 31 answers an empty plist dictionary.
- The code is never logged.
- Out-of-line structure buffers (over 4096 bytes, e.g. selector 31's 32768-byte
  output) arrive unwired, so both struct helpers prepare() the descriptor before
  readBytes/writeBytes, complete() it after, and check the byte count. Without
  that, selector 31 panicked "IOGMD: not wired for the IODMACommand" (ast204).

This file is a shim: the record, the verifier, the backoff and the whole load/verify/set
state machine are in bsd/dev/SweetPasscode.c (2322), which the Mac host tests compile and run.
What is here is the mutex, the copyin, the vnode I/O binding and the selector mapping.
AppleKeyStore::start() calls spc_lock_init(&sweet_pc, &sweet_pc_io, NULL) once, before any
user client exists; without that call the vtable is NULL and the lock silently does nothing.

- Selector 5 is the "turn it on" path and refuses an empty secret or a live code. Selector 15 always proves the old
  code, root included. sweetpasscode=2 clears both slots and reports what actually happened.

Escape hatches: sweetpasscode=0 (today's stub, byte for byte), =2 (clear the
code for this boot), or delete the two files from OrangeFox.

Nothing is encrypted. The HFS root has no cprotect and keychain wraps are still
plaintext; this stops the next person who picks the phone up from using it.

Needs 2322 for bsd/dev/SweetPasscode.h. Security code: plans/passcode/audit-brief.md
is the audit brief, and no image should boot until that audit is a GO.

Check: python3 -I plans/passcode/tools/check-lock.py XNU_DIR (56 checks, 30 negative controls)
Apply: git apply -p1 in the kernel tree.
